Obtaining VAT on Amazon Fees for UK Sellers (post-Aug 2024)
SP-API Reports and Endpoints with Fee VAT Data
GET_VAT_TRANSACTION_DATA (Amazon VAT Transactions Report): Provides detailed information on all VAT-related transactions (sales, returns, refunds, cross-border inventory movements, etc.). This report, available in UK/EU marketplaces, is expected to include Amazon fee charges and their VAT amounts (since those fee charges are part of your VAT transactions post-August 2024). It can be requested via the SP-API Reports endpoint (as reportType = GET_VAT_TRANSACTION_DATA) given the proper authorization (see permissions below).
GET_FLAT_FILE_VAT_INVOICE_DATA_REPORT / GET_XML_VAT_INVOICE_DATA_REPORT: Provide per-order VAT invoice details for sellers enrolled in Amazon’s VAT Calculation Service (VCS). These reports contain all data needed to generate VAT invoices for each shipment, return, or refund of your orders – meant for sellers who let Amazon calculate taxes on customer orders but choose to upload their own invoices. They are available for UK and EU marketplaces. However, these focus on transactional invoices to buyers, not on Amazon’s fees. (In other words, they help you issue invoices to your customers, not to retrieve VAT on Amazon’s charges.) They are relevant only if you use VCS; otherwise, they won’t contain your fee VAT information.
SC_VAT_TAX_REPORT (Amazon VAT Calculation Report): A report providing detailed VAT calculations for buyer shipments, returns, and refunds. This covers the VAT collected on sales (for example, if Amazon is calculating/remitting VAT on customer orders in EU/UK). It is primarily for compliance on the sales side (output VAT) and not directly about the VAT on Amazon fees. It can be requested via SP-API as well, but like the above, it requires special authorization.
Other “VAT Invoice/Tax” Reports: Aside from SP-API, Amazon generates official VAT invoices for the fees it charges you (discussed under Seller Central options). There isn’t a separate SP-API endpoint solely for “VAT on fees invoices” – instead, the VAT Transactions Report (first bullet above) is the comprehensive report that would include those fee-related VAT entries if you have access.
Required API Permissions and Roles
Tax Invoicing (Restricted) Role: The above report types are classified by Amazon as “restricted” reports. According to Amazon’s documentation, you must have the Tax Invoicing (Restricted) developer role enabled to access them. This role is not granted by default – you need to apply for it in your SP-API developer profile (with detailed use-case justification and possibly a compliance review/NDA, since it involves sensitive tax data). Without this role, any attempt to createReport or download these reports will return a 403 “Unauthorized” error (as you experienced).
Restricted Data Token (RDT) Requirement: Even with the appropriate role, when retrieving the actual report document, Amazon requires use of a Restricted Data Token. The GET_VAT_TRANSACTION_DATA and related VAT reports are considered “restricted report types” containing personally identifiable information (PII). Therefore, you must first call the Tokens API (createRestrictedDataToken) specifying the report document you want, and then use the returned RDT when calling getReportDocument. In practice: (1) use createReport to request the VAT report (with your normal access token but having the restricted role), (2) once the report is ready, call createRestrictedDataToken for the report’s document URL, and (3) use the RDT to download the report file.
Additional Permissions/Entitlements: Other SP-API roles (e.g. Finance and Accounting for financial events) are not sufficient to access these VAT reports – the key is the Tax Invoicing (Restricted) role. You do not need a special Seller Central “entitlement” beyond this. Notably, you do not have to be enrolled in Amazon’s VAT services to get fee VAT data via API. The Amazon VAT Transactions Report is available to any seller in the supported marketplaces (UK, DE, FR, IT, ES etc.) who has the API role, regardless of whether they use Amazon’s VAT Calculation Service. (By contrast, the VAT Invoice Data reports explicitly require that you are in the VAT Calculation Service program to have any data to retrieve.) There is no separate “tax services” flag you must enable in Seller Central for fee VAT – the charges began automatically on Aug 1, 2024 for all UK/EU sellers with no opt-in needed. The main hurdle is obtaining API access to the report.
Seller Central Download Options (Including VAT on Fees)
If API access to these reports remains unavailable, you can obtain the necessary VAT-on-fees information through Seller Central’s interfaces:
Seller Fee Invoices in Tax Document Library: Amazon now issues official VAT invoices for the fees it charges you, accessible in your Seller Central account. Navigate to Reports > Tax Document Library > Seller Fee Invoices. Under the Seller Fee Invoices tab, you will find a list of monthly invoices for Amazon’s fees. Each invoice (PDF) corresponds to a period (usually one per month per region) and shows the breakdown of Amazon’s charges and the VAT applied. For example:
Merchant Seller Fees Invoice: covers your Selling on Amazon fees (referral commissions, monthly professional subscription fee, etc.). As of Aug 2024, these invoices show 20% UK VAT added to those fees (for a UK-based seller). If you sell on multiple marketplaces, Amazon may issue separate invoices or sections for different marketplaces – most EU marketplaces also show 20% VAT via the UK entity, with some exceptions like Turkey (.tr) and Poland (.pl) where local VAT is applied at local rates.
FBA Fees Invoices: covers Fulfillment by Amazon fees and related charges (fulfillment fees per order, storage fees, removal/disposal fees, etc.). UK sellers now typically see two FBA invoices each month: one invoice charging 20% VAT for domestic UK FBA transactions (orders fulfilled from UK inventory to UK customers), and a second invoice for zero-rated FBA fees (fees for orders that ship internationally, e.g. from UK to EU or vice versa, which are taxed at 0%). Amazon splits these because under UK tax law, services connected to exports can be zero-rated rather than reverse-charged now. The invoices explicitly state the VAT rate (20% or 0%) applied to each line item.
Other Fee Invoices: If applicable, you might also see invoices for things like Amazon Shipping fees (if you buy shipping labels for MFN orders through Amazon) or other services. The Aug 2024 changes extended VAT to Amazon’s Shipping services for UK sellers as well, so those too would have 20% VAT if Amazon (UK) is the provider. Advertising fees are billed separately (usually by Amazon Advertising in a different portal) and were already subject to local VAT before, so they remain unchanged.
By downloading these PDF invoices from the Tax Document Library, you get the authoritative source of the VAT charged on all Amazon fees. These documents can be used directly for your VAT returns (to support input VAT claims) and for reconciling your accounts. The downside is that this is a manual process (though you can download in bulk or per month). Still, it ensures you have the exact figures. (Tip: Ensure your business information is up-to-date in Seller Central, as Amazon uses that info on the invoices. The invoices will typically list Amazon Services UK as the supplier, your company as the customer, and detail each fee type, net amount, VAT amount, and gross total.)
Settlement Reports / Transaction View: In Seller Central’s Payments section, you can use the Transaction View or generate Date Range Reports to see the transactions and totals of fees over time. For instance, the Transaction View can be filtered by “Service Fees” or “Other” to show what you paid to Amazon, and a Date Range Report will summarize the total amounts paid to Amazon for each fee category over a given period. However, these reports do not break out the VAT component. They will typically show the gross fee amount that was deducted from your balance, and any built-in tax fields (e.g. a tax_amount column in a transaction CSV or the Finances API) remain zero for Amazon fee lines. Essentially, Amazon treats the VAT on fees as outside of the seller’s transaction ledger – the settlement will reflect the total charge (fee + VAT) deducted from your payout, but it won’t label part of it as VAT. So while these reports are useful for cross-checking totals, they cannot be used alone to compute VAT on fees. You would see, for example, a “Fulfillment Fee” of £12 gross where previously it might have been £10 – but the report won’t tell you £2 of that was VAT. This limitation in the Finances data is exactly the gap you’re trying to fill.
Amazon VAT Transactions Report via Seller Central: The VAT Transactions Report (same as GET_VAT_TRANSACTION_DATA) can often be requested from the Tax Document Library as well. Some sellers have access to generate it on demand for a specific date range. If you prefer not to use the API or are waiting on developer access, check the Tax Document Library or Seller Central > Reports > Tax section for an option to create a VAT Transactions Report. If available, you can choose a month or custom date range and download the report (typically a CSV). This report should list, line by line, all transactions relevant to VAT – including your sales (with VAT on customer orders) and Amazon fee charges with their VAT amounts. It’s essentially the manual counterpart to the API report. Generating it in Seller Central might be subject to the same availability (it’s usually available in UK, DE, FR, IT, ES accounts). Keep in mind that, as noted in Amazon’s docs, some reports (like US sales tax reports) must be generated in the UI first, but for EU/UK VAT reports, Amazon indicates they can be requested via API directly. So if you find the option in the UI, that’s a sign you can obtain it immediately without API, and it will contain the fee VAT breakdown you need.
Fallback Options if SP-API Access Remains Blocked
If the dedicated VAT reports are not accessible via SP-API (due to permission issues or time needed to get approval), here are your practical alternatives:
Manual Retrieval of VAT Invoices: The surest fallback is to rely on the official VAT invoices from Seller Central each month. Download the Seller Fee and FBA Fee invoices (PDFs) from the Tax Document Library and use the data therein. While manual, this gives you an exact record of VAT on fees. You can input these figures into your accounting system for VAT reclaim and P&L. For example, you would record an expense for “Amazon Selling Fees” net of VAT, and record the VAT portion separately as input tax – the invoices supply both net and VAT amounts. Many sellers and accountants do this routinely. Ensure you save these invoices for audit purposes (as HMRC will want to see the official VAT invoices from Amazon to support your VAT claims).
Semi-automated “Scraping” or Tools: If you need a more automated approach and SP-API is not viable, some have resorted to programmatically retrieving the invoices. This could involve using a headless browser or scripts to log into Seller Central and download the PDF files or parse the Tax Document Library page. This is not officially supported (and you must handle your credentials securely), but it’s a technical workaround that some advanced users or third-party tools use behind the scenes. Be cautious with this approach to avoid breaching Amazon’s terms of use – if you do implement it, use it only for your own account data.
Leverage Accounting Integrators: Services like A2X, Link My Books, or others have adapted to these changes. In absence of an API feed for VAT on fees, what they do (or recommend) is either ask you to upload the Amazon fee invoices or use the settlement data with assumptions. For example, A2X noted that after August 2024, FBA fee transactions need splitting by destination: fees on UK-fulfilled orders vs. fees on export orders, since one carries 20% VAT and the other 0%. They updated their software to handle two different tax rates on FBA fees. If you use such a tool, ensure it’s configured for the new VAT treatment. If not using an integrator, you can mimic this logic yourself: for any given period, use your order data to identify which FBA fees were domestic (UK) versus international, and allocate VAT accordingly. Important: This method can get complicated, especially if you had mid-period changes or Amazon issued credit notes during the transition. (Amazon did, in fact, issue some one-time credit notes and backdated invoices in Aug 2024 to correct the transition period, which can confuse things.) So, while you can estimate the VAT from raw data – e.g. by taking 1/6 of the gross fee for UK orders as VAT – it’s much safer to reconcile with Amazon’s official numbers. Use this method as a stop-gap to accrue VAT in your accounts, but always true it up to the actual invoices or VAT Transaction Report when available.
No Alternative “Hidden” API: You asked about any unofficial endpoints – aside from the paths discussed (which all require the restricted role), there isn’t a secret SP-API call that sidesteps the restriction. The design is that either you get the GET_VAT_TRANSACTION_DATA report via authorized API, or you pull the data from Seller Central’s provided reports/invoices. Some legacy Amazon MWS reports (for example, there was a “VAT Transactions” report in MWS as well) would have served a similar function, but MWS can no longer be used for EU/UK after the migration deadline. Thus, focusing on the SP-API with proper roles or the manual downloads are the only reliable methods now.
Post–Aug 2024: VAT on Fees Overview and Finances API Behavior
Which Fees Are VAT-applicable: Effective 1 August 2024, Amazon shifted to charging local VAT on nearly all fees for sellers based in the UK (and similarly in EU countries). For a UK business, Amazon’s services (referral fees, marketplace facilitation, FBA services, etc.) are now treated as UK domestic supplies. This means:
Referral Fees (Amazon’s commission on sales) – now plus 20% VAT.
Fulfilment Fees (FBA) – 20% VAT on orders fulfilled within the UK. (As noted, if inventory is shipped from the UK to a buyer outside UK, Amazon considers that fulfillment service zero-rated for VAT, and those are invoiced separately at 0%.)
Monthly Subscription Fee (Professional seller plan £25) – plus 20% VAT (so £30 gross).
Storage Fees (monthly inventory storage charges in UK fulfillment centers) – 20% VAT.
Removal/Disposal Fees (for FBA inventory removals in UK) – 20% VAT.
Inbound Transportation Fees (if you use Amazon’s partnered carrier or Amazon Global Logistics billed through your seller account) – these are Amazon-provided services, now likely with 20% VAT if billed by Amazon UK.
Amazon Shipping fees (buying shipping for self-fulfilled orders via Amazon’s Buy Shipping) – 20% VAT on the shipping label cost (since Amazon is effectively re-selling you postage or courier services; they now charge VAT on it unless it’s a third-party integrated carrier where you pay the carrier directly).
Advertising Fees – no change, because Amazon Advertising fees for UK were already subject to UK VAT (Amazon Advertising UK is a UK entity). If anything, those continue to be invoiced separately from Seller Central.
In summary, most Amazon Seller Central fees that were previously treated under the reverse-charge (for UK sellers) are now standard-rated with VAT. The only major exceptions are when the service is considered supplied outside of the seller’s country (e.g., FBA prep in another country might follow that country’s rules) or exports (e.g., the zero-rated FBA exports scenario). Amazon’s invoices and reports make these distinctions clear (20% vs 0%). For EU sellers, Amazon similarly charges the local VAT of the seller’s country on fees now (e.g., a German seller sees 19% VAT on fees). This change was part of Amazon aligning to local tax regulations and changing the “seller of record” for fees to local entities.
Finances API and Why Tax Amount is 0: Your observation that in the Finances API (financialEvents) the tax_amount for fee lines is always 0 is expected under Amazon’s current data structure. The Finances API (and settlements) historically have separate fields for taxes collected on sales (like VAT/GST from customers) but not for taxes charged on fees. When Amazon charges you a fee plus VAT, they do not split that into two entries in the transaction feed – they simply deduct the total amount from your balance. The VAT component is something Amazon’s accounting handles on their side (remitting it to HMRC), and they provide you the invoice for tax purposes. As a result, the API’s financial events will show the fee transaction with no tax sub-component. For example, if post-August a referral fee of £10 + £2 VAT was charged, the FinancialEvents might only show a “FinancialEvent – ReferralFee = -£12, tax_amount = £0”. This is by design; Amazon hasn’t retrofitted the Finances API to output fee VAT. They expect you to use the VAT reports or invoices to get that breakdown. This is why all your fee tax_amount fields are zero – the system isn’t flagging any portion as “tax” in those feeds. The Amazon VAT Transactions Report is meant to fill that gap by explicitly listing the VAT elements of each transaction (including fees) for compliance. So, in short, you did not miss anything in the Finances API – it simply doesn’t surface VAT on fees. You will need to obtain that information via one of the methods above (API report with proper permissions, or the invoices/reports from Seller Central).
Recommended Path: Decision Tree for Obtaining Fee VAT Data
Finally, to map out a clear path forward, here’s a decision summary:
Preferred Solution – SP-API VAT Reports: If you have the resources and timeframe, apply for Amazon’s “Tax Invoicing (Restricted)” API role for your developer application. Once approved, implement calls to generate and download the GET_VAT_TRANSACTION_DATA report. This will programmatically provide all the VAT on fees data you need, on demand. It’s the most direct and automated solution, ideal for ongoing P&L and VAT filings. (Note: given Amazon’s strict data security requirements, ensure you handle the data per their guidelines. The report will contain sensitive info like order or shipment details related to VAT.)
If you need this data urgently and the role approval is slow, consider reaching out to Amazon via a Developer Support case, explaining the necessity (UK legal requirement to obtain VAT invoices) – sometimes they can expedite such requests or provide guidance.
Alternative – Manual to Semi-automated Process: In the interim or if API access is not granted, plan to retrieve the VAT invoices from Seller Central on a regular basis. For each month, download the Seller Fee Invoice and FBA Fee Invoice(s) PDFs. Enter the VAT amounts from these into your accounting records. This ensures accuracy and compliance (since these are official documents). If you have a developer on your team, you might script this download process as a stop-gap automation (for example, using Puppeteer or Selenium to log in and fetch the PDFs). While not elegant, it can save time versus pure manual work, and no Amazon policy explicitly forbids downloading your own documents.
Cross-verify with Settlements: Continue to use the Finances API or settlement reports for your overall revenue and fees recognition (as you do). Use the totals from those to verify that the sum of net fees plus VAT matches the gross fees deducted. This is a good check to ensure no discrepancies. For instance, you can take a month’s settlement data, sum up all Amazon fee charges, and compare that gross total with the sum on the Amazon fee invoice PDF for that month. They should align (the invoice will show net and VAT that add up to the same gross). This step is mostly for sanity-check and catching any missing items.
If neither API nor manual invoice retrieval is feasible: As a last resort, use your knowledge of which transactions incur VAT to estimate the VAT on fees. For example, you know all your referral fees (UK, EU marketplaces) are 20% VAT – you could calculate 20/120 of those gross fees as VAT. Similarly, partition FBA fees by domestic vs international shipments (perhaps using order fulfillment reports to see which orders were UK-fulfilled domestic vs export) and apply 20% to the domestic portion. This can give you an approximate VAT amount for bookkeeping purposes during the year. However, you should still obtain the official data (via invoices or reports) before finalizing VAT filings, because only Amazon’s documents provide the legally supportable figures and account for edge cases (e.g. Amazon’s rounding, any credits/adjustments like those Aug 2024 credit notes). Use this method only as a temporary measure to avoid blind spots in your P&L.
By following the above decision tree, you’ll secure the data needed to account for VAT on Amazon fees. In summary: get the restricted API access if you can for a long-term automated solution; if not, rely on Seller Central’s Tax Document Library for the official invoices or reports. This will give you a reliable and audit-proof record of VAT on fees, which you can then integrate into your accounting and VAT return preparation with confidence.